1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,487
The CTO's first question from this chapter:
"The deploy job says the hotfix is not

2
00:00:09,527 --> 00:00:14,085
on the server. The engineer says they
pushed it, and their terminal printed

3
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:16,721
Everything up-to-date.
Which one is wrong?"

4
00:00:17,061 --> 00:00:21,025
Neither. The deploy job is right:
the commit is not on the server.

5
00:00:21,065 --> 00:00:24,908
The engineer is right: they ran
a push and Git reported success.

6
00:00:24,948 --> 00:00:30,005
And Git is right: "Everything up-to-date"
is a true statement about the refspec that

7
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:30,672
was pushed.

8
00:00:31,012 --> 00:00:34,300
The whole incident is contained
in one word of that command.

9
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:38,794
By the end of this video you will find it
in under a minute, with three questions

10
00:00:38,834 --> 00:00:42,233
and one request to the server.
Keep your eye on that one word.

11
00:00:44,073 --> 00:00:47,609
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:47,649 --> 00:00:52,534
A ref is a name that points at a commit,
such as a branch, and a remote is your name

13
00:00:52,574 --> 00:00:57,459
for another repository. This is the last
content video of the remotes module, and it

14
00:00:57,499 --> 00:01:02,032
puts a name on something you have been
using since the first one: the refspec.

15
00:01:02,372 --> 00:01:06,300
Every fetch and every push you
have seen was driven by one.

16
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:11,209
git clone wrote one into the configuration.
--single-branch narrowed one.

17
00:01:11,249 --> 00:01:14,169
A bare git push built
one from push.default.

18
00:01:14,509 --> 00:01:19,479
Three parts. First, refspecs as a small
language: read them, write them, widen a

19
00:01:19,519 --> 00:01:24,177
narrow clone, see what the plus sign
does, exclude with a negative refspec.

20
00:01:24,217 --> 00:01:29,250
Second, transports in brief: what the URL
selects, and which two programs talk to

21
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:34,449
each other on the wire. Third, two diagnoses
done from first principles, using only

22
00:01:34,489 --> 00:01:39,710
what this module has taught: "the push pushed
nothing", and "my remote-tracking refs

23
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:40,900
cannot be trusted".

24
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:44,319
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:44,659 --> 00:01:46,071
After this video you can:

26
00:01:46,411 --> 00:01:51,447
Read and write a fetch refspec and a
push refspec, including + and a negative

27
00:01:51,487 --> 00:01:51,975
refspec.

28
00:01:52,315 --> 00:01:55,783
Widen a narrow clone so that a
missing branch becomes fetchable.

29
00:01:56,123 --> 00:02:00,242
Name the transports and trace which
programs a fetch and a push run.

30
00:02:00,582 --> 00:02:04,824
Diagnose "the push pushed nothing" and
"the branch exists on the server and this

31
00:02:04,864 --> 00:02:05,949
clone cannot see it".

32
00:02:07,789 --> 00:02:13,128
Refspecs. In one sentence: a refspec,
optional plus, source, colon, destination, is

33
00:02:13,168 --> 00:02:18,442
the only thing that decides which refs a
fetch or a push touches: the source is on

34
00:02:18,482 --> 00:02:22,071
the sending side, the destination
on the receiving side.

35
00:02:22,411 --> 00:02:27,317
Take that literally. For a fetch, the sender
is the remote, so the source is a ref

36
00:02:27,357 --> 00:02:30,876
name over there and the destination
is a ref name of yours.

37
00:02:30,916 --> 00:02:35,762
For a push, you are the sender, so the
source is a commit expression of yours and

38
00:02:35,802 --> 00:02:38,235
the destination is a ref name over there.

39
00:02:38,575 --> 00:02:44,989
Quick quiz. Take the push refspec HEAD~1:main.
Which side does the word main name

40
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:49,690
there? A, your branch. B, the
server's branch. Your answer?

41
00:02:54,530 --> 00:02:59,504
It's B. On a push, the destination is over
there. Now three lines that deserve a

42
00:02:59,544 --> 00:03:04,770
comment. main alone, on a push, is shorthand
for refs/heads/main:refs/heads/main: my

43
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:09,158
main to their main. An empty source,
colon then a name, is a deletion.

44
00:03:09,198 --> 00:03:14,173
And the plus sign means: allow this update
even when it is not a fast-forward, a

45
00:03:14,213 --> 00:03:15,489
move to a descendant.

46
00:03:15,829 --> 00:03:18,949
Why does the default fetch
refspec carry the plus sign?

47
00:03:18,989 --> 00:03:23,660
Because a remote-tracking ref has to record
the server as it is, rewritten or not.

48
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,935
When does a refspec hurt you? When it
is narrower or wider than you think.

49
00:03:28,975 --> 00:03:33,978
A fetch refspec is configuration that
silently shapes what a clone can see.

50
00:03:34,018 --> 00:03:39,290
A CI clone made with --single-branch has
a refspec for one branch, which is why

51
00:03:39,330 --> 00:03:44,467
other branches are invisible there. And
a mirror-style refspec pasted into an

52
00:03:44,507 --> 00:03:48,569
ordinary clone makes the next
fetch overwrite local branches.

53
00:03:48,609 --> 00:03:50,115
Lab 7.7 ends with that.

54
00:03:50,455 --> 00:03:54,479
Transports. The URL selects how
the two Gits reach each other.

55
00:03:54,519 --> 00:03:57,100
What they say to each other is the same.

56
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:02,546
On the wire, the other side of a fetch is
git-upload-pack, and the other side of a

57
00:04:02,586 --> 00:04:07,755
push is git-receive-pack. Over SSH the same
two programs are started on the server.

58
00:04:08,095 --> 00:04:13,384
Diagnosis. The method for both cases today
is the model from the first video of this

59
00:04:13,424 --> 00:04:18,585
module: three things called main, and only
a few commands that talk to the server.

60
00:04:18,625 --> 00:04:23,724
A push transfers the refs you name, not
"my work". And remote-tracking refs are a

61
00:04:23,764 --> 00:04:24,105
cache.

62
00:04:25,945 --> 00:04:29,960
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy: a mail-forwarding rule.

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,143
"Whatever is addressed to refs/heads/X
over there, file it here as

64
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:35,600
refs/remotes/origin/X."

65
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:40,196
It breaks because the rule runs only
at the moment of a fetch or a push.

66
00:04:40,236 --> 00:04:42,106
Nothing is forwarded in between.

67
00:04:42,446 --> 00:04:47,269
Use the model as a diagnostic habit. When
a clone "cannot see" a branch, the rule

68
00:04:47,309 --> 00:04:52,132
does not cover that name: print the rule.
When a push "did nothing", the rule you

69
00:04:52,172 --> 00:04:56,155
typed selected a ref that had nothing
new: read the rule you typed.

70
00:04:56,195 --> 00:05:00,298
In both cases the command to run
first is not a fetch and not a push.

71
00:05:00,338 --> 00:05:05,282
It is the one that prints the refspec, or
the one that asks the server what it has.

72
00:05:07,122 --> 00:05:11,962
Read it from the top: a plus
sign, a source, a destination.

73
00:05:12,002 --> 00:05:18,332
On a fetch the server sends, so
refs/heads/release/0.1 over there is filed as

74
00:05:18,372 --> 00:05:25,032
refs/remotes/origin/release/0.1 here. With
the narrow refspec, that name finds no

75
00:05:25,072 --> 00:05:30,409
match. On a push you send, so
HEAD~1 is yours and main is theirs.

76
00:05:32,249 --> 00:05:38,328
Part 1: a narrow clone. A clone made with
--single-branch, as a CI job makes it.

77
00:05:38,668 --> 00:05:43,577
Predict the refspec, and the result of the
switch. The branch exists on the server.

78
00:05:48,417 --> 00:05:54,114
The refspec names main only. git switch
release/0.1 fails with "invalid reference".

79
00:05:54,154 --> 00:05:59,368
The clone has no ref of that name and
its fetch rule would never create one.

80
00:05:59,708 --> 00:06:05,830
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Write
the fetch refspec that would let this

81
00:06:05,870 --> 00:06:09,177
clone see release/0.1.
Then say it out loud.

82
00:06:14,017 --> 00:06:17,463
The answer is the second
refspec in the output:

83
00:06:17,503 --> 00:06:23,397
+refs/heads/release/0.1:refs/remotes/origin/release/0.1.
git remote set-branches

84
00:06:23,437 --> 00:06:29,479
--add 🟡 CAUTION, it rewrites configuration,
appends a refspec for one more branch,

85
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:35,191
and set-branches origin "*" restores the
wildcard. Each git fetch then brings

86
00:06:35,231 --> 00:06:41,422
exactly what the configured refspecs cover:
first the release branch, then, with the

87
00:06:41,462 --> 00:06:45,056
wildcard, a dependabot
branch you had never seen.

88
00:06:45,396 --> 00:06:51,267
Part 2: the plus sign. Asha has rewritten
the tip of release/0.1 on the server.

89
00:06:51,307 --> 00:06:55,532
You remove the plus from your
refspec and fetch. Predict.

90
00:07:00,372 --> 00:07:05,870
! [rejected] ... (non-fast-forward), on git
fetch. Your picture of the server stays

91
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:11,341
wrong. With the plus restored, the
remote-tracking ref is forced: plus sign, three

92
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:12,942
dots, "(forced update)".

93
00:07:13,282 --> 00:07:19,362
One careful note from the textbook. The
git fetch manual of 2.55 says updates

94
00:07:19,402 --> 00:07:24,528
outside refs/heads/ and refs/tags/
are accepted without the plus.

95
00:07:24,568 --> 00:07:30,409
This transcript shows that Git 2.55.0
rejects a non-fast-forward update of

96
00:07:30,449 --> 00:07:34,939
refs/remotes/origin/release/0.1
without it. The run wins.

97
00:07:35,279 --> 00:07:37,526
Part 3: a negative refspec.

98
00:07:37,866 --> 00:07:41,807
A negative refspec excludes
matching refs from later fetches.

99
00:07:41,847 --> 00:07:46,375
It does not delete a ref you already
have, hence the git branch -r -d.

100
00:07:46,715 --> 00:07:48,454
Part 4: another namespace.

101
00:07:48,794 --> 00:07:52,400
Refs outside refs/heads/ are
invisible to the default refspec.

102
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:57,223
Name one on the command line to fetch it
into a local branch, or map the namespace

103
00:07:57,263 --> 00:08:01,987
permanently. The last command shows the
three configured lines: the wildcard, the

104
00:08:02,027 --> 00:08:03,575
exclusion, and the mapping.

105
00:08:03,915 --> 00:08:09,272
refs/pull/<number>/head is how GitHub exposes
the head commit of every pull request.

106
00:08:09,312 --> 00:08:14,476
Its documentation gives git fetch origin
pull/ID/head:BRANCH_NAME and states that

107
00:08:14,516 --> 00:08:19,616
the namespace is read-only: a push to it
is answered with a remote rejected line

108
00:08:19,656 --> 00:08:24,563
saying "deny updating a hidden ref". gh
pr checkout <number> wraps the fetch.

109
00:08:24,603 --> 00:08:29,446
Described from the documentation. The
lab server here only imitates the ref.

110
00:08:29,786 --> 00:08:34,072
Part 5: push refspecs. Two
commits on main. git push 🟡.

111
00:08:34,412 --> 00:08:38,663
Three push refspecs. Publish
everything except your newest commit.

112
00:08:38,703 --> 00:08:44,060
Create a branch under a different name. And
delete it, which is 🔴 as every deletion

113
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:48,221
on a server. Status then shows
the one commit that stayed local.

114
00:08:48,561 --> 00:08:53,518
Part 6: transports.
labs/run ch12/transports.

115
00:08:53,858 --> 00:08:58,944
git url-parse, Git 2.55 or later,
shows how Git reads a URL.

116
00:08:58,984 --> 00:09:03,387
The form
git@github.com:acme/support-bot.git is SSH.

117
00:09:03,427 --> 00:09:08,684
A plain path is not a URL at all:
it names a local repository.

118
00:09:09,024 --> 00:09:12,430
labs/run ch12/transport-trace.

119
00:09:12,770 --> 00:09:15,854
git-upload-pack for a fetch,
git-receive-pack for a push.

120
00:09:16,194 --> 00:09:21,670
GIT_TRACE_PACKET=1 prints the conversation.
fetch< is received, and fetch> is sent.

121
00:09:21,710 --> 00:09:25,724
The server announces protocol
version 2 and its capabilities.

122
00:09:25,764 --> 00:09:31,307
The client asks for refs with ls-refs,
limited by ref-prefix lines derived from your

123
00:09:31,347 --> 00:09:36,026
refspecs, so a server with very many
refs lists only those you can use.

124
00:09:36,066 --> 00:09:41,608
Then want names what you need and have what
you already hold, and the server answers

125
00:09:41,648 --> 00:09:46,593
with a packfile of the difference, one
file that holds the missing objects.

126
00:09:52,933 --> 00:09:58,098
A push is shorter: the advertisement, one
"old, new, ref" command, the pack, and the

127
00:09:58,138 --> 00:10:00,391
report unpack ok, ok refs/heads/main.

128
00:10:00,731 --> 00:10:03,467
Back to the transports demo
for the last transport.

129
00:10:09,007 --> 00:10:12,017
A bundle is a file that
plays the part of a remote.

130
00:10:12,057 --> 00:10:15,246
You can clone or fetch from
it. You cannot push to it.

131
00:10:15,586 --> 00:10:20,275
Part 7: "the commit exists
locally but not on the server".

132
00:10:20,315 --> 00:10:22,151
labs/run ch12/diagnose.

133
00:10:22,491 --> 00:10:27,128
That is the hook. Try it now, thirty
seconds, on paper: before the next snippet,

134
00:10:27,168 --> 00:10:30,403
write down the three questions
you would ask. I'll wait.

135
00:10:35,243 --> 00:10:41,021
Question one, which branches contain the
commit: one local branch, hotfix/timeout,

136
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:46,840
and no remote-tracking branch. Question
two, does that branch have an upstream: ##

137
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:52,729
hotfix/timeout with nothing after it means
no. Question three, which commits has no

138
00:10:52,769 --> 00:10:58,406
remote-tracking ref ever seen: git log
--branches --not --remotes lists c19ab53.

139
00:10:58,746 --> 00:11:01,804
And the server, asked directly,
has no ref at that ID.

140
00:11:02,144 --> 00:11:06,572
Here's the whole incident in one
picture, with its branch names.

141
00:11:06,612 --> 00:11:12,366
main and origin/main sit on 510ee94. The
hotfix, c19ab53, is on hotfix/timeout, and

142
00:11:12,406 --> 00:11:16,415
nowhere else. So git push origin
main had nothing to send.

143
00:11:16,455 --> 00:11:17,951
The one word was main.

144
00:11:18,291 --> 00:11:22,811
And the root-cause box for the hook. Read
its prevention line: the last lines of

145
00:11:22,851 --> 00:11:25,547
push output name the source
and the destination.

146
00:11:30,887 --> 00:11:35,062
After the fix, a remote-tracking branch
contains the commit, and the "not on any

147
00:11:35,102 --> 00:11:36,221
remote" list is empty.

148
00:11:36,561 --> 00:11:41,057
The same three questions sort out the other
causes of this symptom: a rejected push

149
00:11:41,097 --> 00:11:45,538
whose exit status a script ignored, a push
to the wrong remote, a commit made on a

150
00:11:45,578 --> 00:11:50,074
detached HEAD, or a reader who looked at a
stale remote-tracking ref instead of the

151
00:11:50,114 --> 00:11:50,457
server.

152
00:11:50,797 --> 00:11:54,005
Part 8: "my remote-tracking
refs cannot be trusted".

153
00:11:54,345 --> 00:11:58,749
A local branch named origin/main,
usually created by a mistyped command.

154
00:11:58,789 --> 00:12:03,563
Git warns that the name is ambiguous and
then resolves it to the local branch,

155
00:12:03,603 --> 00:12:07,699
because refs/heads/ comes before
refs/remotes/ in the lookup order.

156
00:12:07,739 --> 00:12:11,711
Every comparison "against origin/main"
now uses the wrong commit.

157
00:12:11,751 --> 00:12:16,093
In git branch -a the impostor is the
entry without the remotes/ prefix.

158
00:12:16,433 --> 00:12:22,026
Here are the two names side by side: the
remote-tracking ref on 510ee94, and the

159
00:12:22,066 --> 00:12:23,927
impostor branch on 95671d3.

160
00:12:24,267 --> 00:12:29,244
When the remote-tracking refs themselves
are in doubt, remember what they are: a

161
00:12:29,284 --> 00:12:34,511
cache. Delete them all, 🟡, and fetch. Local
branches and their upstream settings are

162
00:12:34,551 --> 00:12:39,214
not touched. Status says [gone] in
between and is correct again afterwards.

163
00:12:39,254 --> 00:12:44,231
The price is the reflogs of the deleted
refs, your only record of earlier server

164
00:12:44,271 --> 00:12:44,669
states.

165
00:12:46,509 --> 00:12:48,158
Five mistakes to watch for.

166
00:12:48,498 --> 00:12:52,283
Reading "Everything up-to-date"
as "my commit is on the server".

167
00:12:52,323 --> 00:12:57,305
Root cause: a push transfers the refs you
name; the refspec main had nothing to send

168
00:12:57,345 --> 00:12:59,636
while the commit sat on another branch.

169
00:12:59,976 --> 00:13:05,174
Concluding that a branch does not exist
because git switch says "invalid reference"

170
00:13:05,214 --> 00:13:10,222
in a CI workspace. Root cause: the clone's
fetch refspec names one branch, so no

171
00:13:10,262 --> 00:13:13,440
remote-tracking ref for the
other was ever created.

172
00:13:13,780 --> 00:13:17,134
Removing the plus sign from a
fetch refspec "for safety".

173
00:13:17,174 --> 00:13:21,303
Root cause: without it a rewritten
branch is rejected on fetch and the

174
00:13:21,343 --> 00:13:25,412
remote-tracking ref keeps recording
a state the server no longer has.

175
00:13:25,752 --> 00:13:29,566
Adding a negative refspec and
expecting existing refs to vanish.

176
00:13:29,606 --> 00:13:34,323
Root cause: it excludes refs from later
fetches and deletes nothing you already

177
00:13:34,363 --> 00:13:34,624
have.

178
00:13:34,964 --> 00:13:39,736
Creating a local branch called origin/main.
Root cause: refs/heads/ is looked up

179
00:13:39,776 --> 00:13:44,549
before refs/remotes/, so the name resolves
to the impostor, with only a warning.

180
00:13:46,389 --> 00:13:50,683
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform
team trains on an air-gapped cluster.

181
00:13:50,723 --> 00:13:53,830
There is no route from the
cluster to the Git server.

182
00:13:53,870 --> 00:13:58,699
The training code still has to arrive as
a Git repository, with history, so that a

183
00:13:58,739 --> 00:14:01,964
model artifact can record the
commit it was built from.

184
00:14:02,304 --> 00:14:06,999
The transport for that is a bundle. Run
git bundle create on the connected side,

185
00:14:07,039 --> 00:14:11,912
with git bundle verify printing the refs
it contains and confirming that it records

186
00:14:11,952 --> 00:14:16,883
a complete history. Carry the file across.
Clone or fetch from it on the other side.

187
00:14:16,923 --> 00:14:19,251
In the clone, origin is the bundle file.

188
00:14:19,591 --> 00:14:24,600
For ordinary remotes, GitHub
accepts HTTPS and SSH URLs.

189
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:32,085
According to its blog, it stopped accepting
account passwords for Git over HTTPS on

190
00:14:32,125 --> 00:14:39,298
13 August 2021 and removed the unauthenticated
git:// protocol on 15 March 2022.

191
00:14:39,338 --> 00:14:42,274
Chapter 16 covers authentication.

192
00:14:42,614 --> 00:14:48,063
And the textbook's rule for refspec problems,
which belongs on a team runbook: when

193
00:14:48,103 --> 00:14:53,288
a clone "cannot see" a branch or "keeps
losing" one, print git config get --all

194
00:14:53,328 --> 00:14:55,008
remote.origin.fetch first.

195
00:14:56,848 --> 00:15:03,578
Your turn. Do Lab 7.7, "Refspec
surgery", in lab-manual/m07-remotes.md.

196
00:15:03,918 --> 00:15:05,338
Predict before each fetch:

197
00:15:05,678 --> 00:15:10,972
Given the configured refspecs, which refs
will this fetch create, move or leave

198
00:15:11,012 --> 00:15:11,377
alone?

199
00:15:11,717 --> 00:15:16,515
If the server's branch was rewritten, will
the fetch accept or reject the update,

200
00:15:16,555 --> 00:15:17,709
and what decides it?

201
00:15:18,049 --> 00:15:22,665
For the last step of the lab: which of your
refs is the destination of the refspec

202
00:15:22,705 --> 00:15:25,731
you are about to paste? Answer
that before you run it.

203
00:15:26,071 --> 00:15:30,522
The challenge is Incident 10,
incidents/10-commit-local-not-remote.

204
00:15:30,562 --> 00:15:32,399
Read the symptoms file only.

205
00:15:34,239 --> 00:15:39,478
Q216: "In a CI workspace git switch
release/2.3 fails with an invalid reference

206
00:15:39,518 --> 00:15:45,092
although the branch exists on the server,
and on a laptop a fetch rejects the update

207
00:15:45,132 --> 00:15:50,505
of a remote-tracking ref as a non-fast-forward.
Diagnose both from what a refspec

208
00:15:50,545 --> 00:15:50,772
is."

209
00:15:53,112 --> 00:15:58,385
Answer out loud first. A strong answer begins
by defining a refspec in one sentence,

210
00:15:58,425 --> 00:16:02,306
with its three parts, and then
uses that one definition twice.

211
00:16:02,346 --> 00:16:07,365
For each symptom it says which part of
the refspec is responsible, which command

212
00:16:07,405 --> 00:16:11,475
prints the evidence, and which
command changes the configuration.

213
00:16:11,515 --> 00:16:14,890
It also says how each clone
came to have that refspec.

214
00:16:14,930 --> 00:16:20,013
Two symptoms, one concept: if your answer
needs two unrelated explanations, start

215
00:16:20,053 --> 00:16:20,392
again.

216
00:16:22,232 --> 00:16:25,160
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

217
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:30,525
A refspec maps source refs on the sending
side to destination refs on the receiving

218
00:16:30,565 --> 00:16:34,004
side, and the plus sign allows
a non-fast-forward update.

219
00:16:34,044 --> 00:16:38,521
A clone can only see the branches its
fetch refspec covers, and git remote

220
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:43,647
set-branches widens it. The URL selects the
transport, and on the wire a fetch talks

221
00:16:43,687 --> 00:16:48,652
to git-upload-pack and a push to
git-receive-pack. A bundle is a remote in a file.

222
00:16:48,992 --> 00:16:54,108
"Everything up-to-date" is true of the
refspec that was pushed, so I locate a commit

223
00:16:54,148 --> 00:16:58,895
with git branch -a --contains, check the
upstream, and ask the server with git

224
00:16:58,935 --> 00:16:59,509
ls-remote.

225
00:16:59,849 --> 00:17:03,611
Remote-tracking refs are a cache that
can be deleted and fetched again.

226
00:17:05,451 --> 00:17:12,722
Read sections 12.12 to 12.17 of Chapter
12, and do the Practice section 12.19.

227
00:17:13,062 --> 00:17:17,899
That's the remotes module, complete. You
can read a refspec, and you can find a

228
00:17:17,939 --> 00:17:21,603
commit that was "pushed" and
isn't there, in under a minute.

229
00:17:21,643 --> 00:17:26,233
Do Lab 7.7 while this is fresh. Next
time: the gate briefing for branching.

230
00:17:26,273 --> 00:17:29,444
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

231
00:17:29,484 --> 00:17:30,925
